The task is a single ciphertext-by-scalar-plaintext multiply, which already costs one plaintext multiply and one rescale. There is no way to avoid the multiplication, because the scalar `s` is only available as a plaintext input and can't be applied without a multiply.

The one change I can try is a different operation order: `s * x0` instead of `x0 * s`. CHEHAB will probably normalize this to the same circuit, so it may not count as a new circuit and may not win. I also considered computing `x0 + x0` or similar, but that doesn't give `x0 * s`.

I'm submitting the commuted form as the only option, and I don't expect a measurable improvement.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * s;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

My previous message said I was submitting the commuted form `s * x0`, but the code I gave was the unchanged baseline. That is why it was reported as identical.

I don't see a cheaper circuit. The output needs the encrypted `x0` scaled by a value that exists only as a plaintext input. That takes one plaintext multiply and its rescale, and every alternative I can build (such as `(x0+x0)*s - x0*s`) adds operations. The kernel below is the commuted form, which I expect CHEHAB to normalize to the same circuit as the baseline, so it probably can't win.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = s * x0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't have a cheaper circuit to offer. `x0 * s` is already the minimum: one plaintext multiply and one rescale, with no ciphertext multiplies, rotations, or alignments. Any correct rewrite I can construct only adds operations. For example, `(x0+x0)*s - x0*s` gives the right result but needs two plaintext multiplies. The commuted form was already reported as the same circuit as the baseline, so reordering the operands can't help either.

I'm resubmitting the baseline kernel as my best answer. It will be reported as identical to the baseline again.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * s;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I still see no cheaper circuit, so this attempt is a different structure that I expect to be slower, not faster. It computes `(x0+x0)*s - x0*s`, which equals `x0*s` but uses two plaintext multiplies. I'm submitting it only to see whether CHEHAB simplifies it into something different from the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext a = x0 + x0;
  Ciphertext b = a * s;
  Ciphertext c = x0 * s;
  Ciphertext y = b - c;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

As expected, that was slower (18.60 ms against 8.81 ms): it used two plaintext multiplies and two rescales. The baseline is still the best I have.

